WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.152
Hello and welcome everyone!

00:00:01.152 --> 00:00:02.303
Hope you're&nbsp;having a great day.

00:00:02.303 --> 00:00:03.359
And welcome to mCoding,

00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:07.360
where we try to get just a little bit&nbsp;
better at programming every episode.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.720
Let's just jump into it today 
and&nbsp;start off with a question for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.078
Suppose you're given two or more dictionaries&nbsp;
containing corresponding data.

00:00:15.078 --> 00:00:18.682
By corresponding data, I mean they
 share the same set of keys.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:22.255
In&nbsp;this case, the keys are some channel
 IDs for some of my favorite YouTube channels.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:26.080
And the values&nbsp;are names and 
subscriber counts of those channels.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.112
Take a moment and let me know 
in the comments&nbsp;below

00:00:28.112 --> 00:00:31.711
how you would iterate over these 
corresponding dictionaries.

00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:34.960
While you're doing&nbsp;that, 
let me motivate this example a little bit.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.314
Because the first question you 
might have&nbsp;might be:

00:00:37.314 --> 00:00:42.308
Why do we have the names and 
sub_counts stored in separate dictionaries?

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:47.760
You might&nbsp;be expecting 
something more structured like this.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.308
In this example, we've defined a class that&nbsp;
stores the ID, name, and sub_count of a channel.

00:00:53.308 --> 00:00:57.360
And then we have this structured data&nbsp;
of ChannelData stored in a list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.683
If you got this data from 
a web API,

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:02.272
this&nbsp;is what you might be 
expecting to get back.

00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:06.720
An aggregate of structured data types that&nbsp;each 
contain many different types of data.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.880
You can then have a dictionary 
of IDs&nbsp;to the channels they correspond to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.040
And then the preferred way to loop 
is to&nbsp;loop over the items of that dictionary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.795
This is certainly a common case. 
But&nbsp;it's not the only case.

00:01:17.795 --> 00:01:21.614
For instance, if the names came 
from one API like the YouTube&nbsp;Data API

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:26.118
but the subscriber counts came from a
 different API like the Analytics API,

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:29.350
then you&nbsp;could very well end 
up with two separate data structures

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.227
that have the same keys.

00:01:31.227 --> 00:01:36.320
And that's&nbsp;how we end up with 
the situation from before.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.778
Whether you got these pieces of 
data from&nbsp;separate sources

00:01:38.778 --> 00:01:40.718
and your goal is to combine them

00:01:40.718 --> 00:01:43.457
or whether it just makes more sense&nbsp;
in the context of your program

00:01:43.457 --> 00:01:46.380
to keep them separate and you just&nbsp;
want to loop over both of them,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.840
the question remains: how should you&nbsp;
loop over the corresponding data?

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.974
Here's one way to do it. 
It works but it's not the&nbsp;best way.

00:01:53.974 --> 00:01:56.511
Loop over the channel IDs (cid)
from the names.

00:01:56.511 --> 00:01:59.943
Then use the channel ID (cid) to pull 
out the name&nbsp;and sub_count.

00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:03.951
But the first thing I want to note is 
this is actually an anti-pattern of&nbsp;for-loops.

00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:05.923
Whenever you loop over the 
keys of a dictionary

00:02:05.923 --> 00:02:09.629
and then the first thing you do&nbsp;is 
get out the value corresponding to that key,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:13.052
you should instead prefer to 
 loop over the items&nbsp;of that dictionary.

00:02:13.052 --> 00:02:14.408
This is going to be slightly more efficient

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:18.086
because when you loop over items,&nbsp;
you don't have to compute the hash of the keys.

00:02:18.086 --> 00:02:20.300
This method is also fine
 and it works.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.731
But&nbsp;it's still not quite what 
I would like.

00:02:22.731 --> 00:02:25.591
The names and sub_counts are supposed to 
 have&nbsp;corresponding data.

00:02:25.591 --> 00:02:29.228
But if there was a missing subscriber
 count, I would get a key error.

00:02:29.228 --> 00:02:34.224
Whereas,&nbsp;if there was a missing name, I would 
silently just loop over less data.

00:02:34.224 --> 00:02:37.091
This kind of asymmetry&nbsp;
can cause very subtle bugs.

00:02:37.091 --> 00:02:39.630
Additionally, the code just looks 
 a little bit asymmetric.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.925
And that can cause it to be a little bit
 harder for people to read.

00:02:42.925 --> 00:02:46.640
Here I'm looping over&nbsp;names, 
why not loop over sub_counts instead?

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.515
Even though they share kind of equal 
purpose&nbsp;in this iteration,

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:51.667
I had to pick one of them 
 to go first.

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:54.768
So while it works, this is still&nbsp;not 
quite what I'm looking for.

00:02:54.768 --> 00:02:56.565
To get a better feel for what 
I am looking for

00:02:56.565 --> 00:03:01.612
Let's&nbsp;take inspiration from
 a very similar example.

00:03:01.612 --> 00:03:06.649
Suppose that instead of dictionaries,
 these data were stored in corresponding lists.

00:03:06.649 --> 00:03:11.105
In that case, the simple and straightforward way 
to do it is to use the built-in `zip` function.

00:03:11.105 --> 00:03:16.825
As of Python 3.10, you could even use the `strict` flag 
to ensure that all the lists have the same length.

00:03:16.825 --> 00:03:19.825
I think, this is a very clear and 
great way to do it for lists.

00:03:19.825 --> 00:03:21.747
So, how do we zip dictionaries?

00:03:21.747 --> 00:03:28.072
We can't just use `zip` itself because
 `zip` is going to loop over the keys of dictionaries.

00:03:28.072 --> 00:03:29.926
We could try to zip corresponding items.

00:03:29.926 --> 00:03:32.344
But there are a lot of problems 
with this approach.

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.517
We end up repeating the ID field.

00:03:34.517 --> 00:03:36.738
We now need extra parentheses.

00:03:36.738 --> 00:03:38.214
And we're actually depending on 
the fact that

00:03:38.214 --> 00:03:41.595
the keys were inserted into 
the dictionaries in the same order.

00:03:41.595 --> 00:03:46.508
As of Python 3.7, dictionary iteration 
order is guaranteed to be insertion order.

00:03:46.508 --> 00:03:49.594
But since these dictionaries came from
 potentially separate sources,

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:52.736
we can't really assume that the keys
 were inserted in the same order

00:03:52.736 --> 00:03:55.111
even if we know that they have 
the same keys.

00:03:55.111 --> 00:03:58.925
What I really want is to be able 
 to write something like this.

00:03:58.925 --> 00:04:01.232
I want a dictionary version of `zip` (dict_zip),

00:04:01.232 --> 00:04:02.217
And when I loop over it,

00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:06.664
I get out the key and then value, value, 
value for each of the corresponding dictionaries.

00:04:06.664 --> 00:04:10.061
To me this API is simple
 and straightforward, just like `zip`.

00:04:10.061 --> 00:04:12.438
The only problem is there 
 is no `dict_zip`.

00:04:12.438 --> 00:04:16.563
It's not a built-in like `zip` and it's not
 part of the itertools library either.

00:04:16.563 --> 00:04:20.240
But we're all programmers here. 
If we want it, we can write it ourselves.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.563
So let's write it.
Take in the dictionaries.

00:04:22.563 --> 00:04:23.810
We're going to make this 
 function a generator.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.771
So let's just throw in a yield 
 so that we have that mindset.

00:04:26.771 --> 00:04:31.419
If we weren't passing any dictionaries, 
 then we just stop.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:42.666
I personally like the strictness to be built in. 
 So, let's do a length check.

00:04:42.666 --> 00:04:53.362
Then we go ahead and loop over corresponding 
 keys in the most efficient way that we can.

00:04:53.362 --> 00:04:56.412
That means using the items method 
 of the zeroth dictionary.

00:04:56.412 --> 00:05:00.776
And then just grabbing out the rest of the values from 
 the other dictionaries in a comprehension.

00:05:00.776 --> 00:05:03.716
Here I'm using a generator 
 comprehension to get all the values.

00:05:03.716 --> 00:05:07.188
Then the star is used to unpack all the
 values so that the result is a tuple.

00:05:07.188 --> 00:05:11.087
So all in all, we have a tuple of the key, 
first value, and then the rest of the values.

00:05:11.087 --> 00:05:13.836
And that's all there is to it!

00:05:13.836 --> 00:05:16.766
Now we can loop over corresponding dictionaries 
 in a straightforward way.

00:05:16.766 --> 00:05:20.763
And all the asymmetry that we had before 
 is hidden inside the implementation.

00:05:20.763 --> 00:05:24.000
There are a few other things that I want 
 to say about this implementation though.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.610
First off, this is a prime example of something 
 that really should be implemented in C.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:33.256
In Python, we're forced to use brackets 
 in order to get the corresponding values for the keys.

00:05:33.256 --> 00:05:36.335
Every time we do this, this involves 
 computing the hash of the key.

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:41.490
But the hash of the key is the same 
for every dictionary, so why recompute it?

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.000
This is from the CPython source code.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.689
It's in the file dictobject.c, which defines 
the implementation of a dictionary.

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:53.933
In particular, in C, we can use this 
 `GetItem_KnownHash` function.

00:05:53.933 --> 00:05:57.125
This function is special built for getting 
 a key out of a dictionary

00:05:57.125 --> 00:05:59.390
when you already know the hash.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.217
So our `dictionary_zip` is a perfect 
 example of a place

00:06:02.217 --> 00:06:07.119
where we could save a lot of computation time 
 by removing the need to recompute a hash function.

00:06:07.119 --> 00:06:10.406
This is something I could say more about in 
 a future video if you're interested.

00:06:10.406 --> 00:06:13.163
But I claim that if we did this 
 using the C API,

00:06:13.163 --> 00:06:17.669
we could get away with not computing
 the hash function at all for any key.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:23.543
There's also one other hidden usability 
issue in this implementation:

00:06:23.543 --> 00:06:31.082
Look what happens if I try to use autocomplete 
 on one of these entries (say, the channel ID).

00:06:31.082 --> 00:06:33.622
I get nothing useful!

00:06:33.622 --> 00:06:35.230
Not everyone depends on autocomplete,.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.792
But having it there is a huge 
 win for most developers.

00:06:38.792 --> 00:06:42.390
And the way we wrote our implementation 
 is not amenable to autocomplete.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.200
Of course, if this was part of 
 the standard library,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.695
we wouldn't have to worry about this. 
 It would be taken care of.

00:06:46.695 --> 00:06:50.060
But a little tip when you try to implement 
 these things in one of your own code bases:

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:53.646
you can help autocomplete work 
 better by using type hints.

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.902
Unfortunately, if you think about 
 what the signature of this function should be,

00:06:56.902 --> 00:06:59.794
Python's type hinting system 
 does not yet support it.

00:06:59.794 --> 00:07:03.926
There's an early draft of a PEP 
 to fix this in 3.12 or 3.13.

00:07:03.926 --> 00:07:05.718
But for now here's a quick fix.

00:07:05.718 --> 00:07:09.965
The quick fix in this case is to provide 
 typing overloads for the most common use cases.

00:07:09.965 --> 00:07:14.350
I'd say that zipping one, two or three
 dictionaries together are the most common cases.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.282
So make all the type variables 
 you need.

00:07:16.282 --> 00:07:18.670
And then just provide overloads 
 for each of those cases.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.486
We actually don't even use dictionaries; 
 any mapping will do.

00:07:21.486 --> 00:07:24.000
So here's an overload that takes 
 in two mappings.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.000
They share the same key type. 
 But they can have different value types.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.533
Then I'll return an iterator of tuples 
 of the common key, value 1 and value 2.

00:07:31.533 --> 00:07:35.172
Let's go back down to our example 
 and see how the autocomplete works now.

00:07:35.172 --> 00:07:37.202
Here we are again 
 and let's see what it does.

00:07:37.202 --> 00:07:42.660
Perfect! This time I'm getting string 
autocomplete suggestions for the string key.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.688
And for the sub_count which is an 
 integer we're getting `int` suggestions.

00:07:46.688 --> 00:07:51.000
Finally, don't be afraid to go wild and to 
 find ways of iterating that work for your code base.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.555
Maybe you just want to loop 
 over common elements of a dictionary

00:07:53.555 --> 00:07:55.623
like a SQL inner join.

00:07:55.623 --> 00:08:00.055
Or maybe you want to loop over a union
 of keys like an outer join and provide a fillvalue.

00:08:00.055 --> 00:08:04.946
In the right circumstances, providing these functions 
 can actually vastly improve the readability of your code.

00:08:04.946 --> 00:08:08.352
And that's what I recommend you do. Think 
 about the ways that you iterate.

00:08:08.352 --> 00:08:12.407
Is there anything that you do often by
 hand that you could just make a function for?

00:08:12.407 --> 00:08:14.167
If so, do it!

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:16.288
Anyway, thanks for watching! 
 That's all I've got.

00:08:16.288 --> 00:08:19.633
Thank you to my patrons and donors.
 And I'll see you in the next one!

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:23.333
As always, don't forget to slap that like 
 button an odd number of times.

